[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Local Data Storage in Xamarin: SQLite, Realm, and File System"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Choosing the right local data storage mechanism is crucial for building robust cross-platform applications in Xamarin. Depending on your app needs—whether it is simple key-value pairs, complex relational data, or high-performance object mapping—Xamarin provides multiple avenues for local persistence."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Direct File System Access"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"For storing raw text, configuration files, or cached media, the device file system is the most direct approach. Xamarin applications can leverage standard .NET System.IO APIs combined with Xamarin.Essentials for cross-platform path resolution."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"using System.IO;
using Xamarin.Essentials;

// Get the application data folder path
string folderPath = FileSystem.AppDataDirectory;
string filePath = Path.Combine(folderPath, "settings.txt");

// Write text to a file
File.WriteAllText(filePath, "Theme=Dark");

// Read text from a file
if (File.Exists(filePath))
{
    string settings = File.ReadAllText(filePath);
}"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"2. SQLite via SQLite-net"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"SQLite is a lightweight, embedded relational database engine. In Xamarin, the most popular way to interact with SQLite is through the sqlite-net-pcl NuGet package, which uses ORM (Object-Relational Mapping) features to map C# classes to database tables."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Install the sqlite-net-pcl and SQLitePCLRaw.bundle_green NuGet packages into your shared project."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Define your data model using attributes like [PrimaryKey] and [AutoIncrement]."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"using SQLite;

public class User
{
    [PrimaryKey, AutoIncrement]
    public int Id { get; set; }
    public string Name { get; set; }
    public int Age { get; set; }
}

// Database connection and operations
string dbPath = Path.Combine(FileSystem.AppDataDirectory, "users.db3");
var db = new SQLiteConnection(dbPath);

// Create table
db.CreateTable<User>();

// Insert a new record
var newUser = new User { Name = "John Doe", Age = 30 };
db.Insert(newUser);

// Query records
var query = db.Table<User>().Where(u => u.Age >= 18).ToList();"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Realm Database"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Realm is a modern, object-oriented database built specifically for mobile applications. Unlike SQLite, Realm does not require an ORM layer because objects are persisted directly as native Realm objects, offering superior performance for complex object graphs and reactive queries."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Install the Realm NuGet package in your Xamarin project."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Inherit your models from RealmObject and use the [MapTo] or [PrimaryKey] attributes."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"using Realms;

public class TaskItem : RealmObject
{
    [PrimaryKey]
    public string Id { get; set; } = Guid.NewGuid().ToString();
    public string Title { get; set; }
    public bool IsCompleted { get; set; }
}

// Open a Realm instance
var realm = Realm.Instance();

// Write data
realm.Write(() =>
{
    realm.Add(new TaskItem
    {
        Title = "Learn Xamarin Local Storage",
        IsCompleted = false
    });
});

// Query data reactively
var tasks = realm.All<TaskItem>().Where(t => t.IsCompleted == false);"},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Summary and Best Practices"},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use the File System for unstructured data, logs, large binary assets, and documents."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use SQLite for standard relational data requirements with traditional SQL query needs."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use Realm for high-performance applications with complex, highly-nested object relationships and reactive UI patterns."}]
